silm:instances_papers_02_music_sections_03_construction_dag_yoneda_hom_leg__leg_4 a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "'] \\& |[schemanode]| TaskGraph tikzcd% The Music construction graph as a path in the path category \\(PMusic\\): the long publication path from source packet to page section. Each step is a typed arrow; skipping any step requires an explicit identity arrow that commutes.fig:music-construction-path theorem[Construction factorization (categorical form)] thm:music-construction-factorization Let \\(PMusic\\) be the path category generated by \\(ingest,type,validate,schedule,execute,witness,publish\\). Then the publication morphism \\[ p=publish \\] is the unique admissible factorization in \\(PMusic\\) from \\(SourcePacket\\) to \\(PageSection\\). theorem proof Step 1. Each generator is a typed arrow in \\(PMusic\\) with a declared source and target. Step 2. The path category contains every composite of generators; \\(p\\) is the composite of all seven generators in the declared order. Step 3. Any other morphism from \\(SourcePacket\\) to \\(PageSection\\) that omits a generator either misses a type, gate, task, or witness node --- making the omission a missing arrow in the constructed DAG. Step 4 (anchor). For the Mittens quality observation: ingest the body-mass record, type it as a quality claim, validate against the measurement shape, schedule the quality-section render task, execute it, witness by the trace, publish the quality section. Step 5 (Yoneda). By Yoneda on \\(PMusic\\), the profile of \\(PageSection\\) recovers the full factorization chain. Step 6. The construction factorization is the unique admissible path. proof Diagram law (Figure~fig:music-construction-path): the top row and the bottom row of the diagram represent the same publication path; skipping the \\(execute\\) step leaves the bottom \\(TaskGraph\\) arrow without a target. [0.88]% tikzcd[column sep=large,row sep=large,ampersand replacement=\\&] |[schemanode]| TypedClaim [r," .
